        "---\n",
        "title: \"Ethics, Bias, and Mathematical Responsibility\"\n",
        "subtitle: \"Fairness, uncertainty, privacy, and responsible NLP deployment\"\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.chapter-opening}\n",
        "Modern NLP systems are mathematical systems deployed in social environments.  A language model is trained on data, optimized under a loss function, evaluated by metrics, and then used by people.  Ethical analysis therefore cannot be separated from mathematical analysis.  Before we can ask whether a system is fair, safe, or trustworthy, we must specify the random variables, the population, the representation, the target, the loss, the metric, and the deployment setting.\n",
        "\n",
        "This chapter develops a mathematical language for responsibility in NLP.  The goal is not to reduce ethics to formulas.  Rather, the goal is to make assumptions visible.  A formula can clarify what is being measured, what is being ignored, and where statistical uncertainty enters.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "## 25.1 Learning goals\n",
        "\n",
        "After reading this chapter, students should be able to:\n",
        "\n",
        "- formulate bias, fairness, uncertainty, and privacy questions using random variables and distributions;\n",
        "- distinguish sampling bias, measurement bias, label bias, representation bias, optimization bias, and deployment bias;\n",
        "- compute common group fairness metrics from a confusion matrix;\n",
        "- explain why different fairness metrics can conflict;\n",
        "- analyze distribution shift using training and deployment distributions;\n",
        "- compute calibration error and understand why accuracy alone is insufficient;\n",
        "- use embedding association scores to audit representation bias;\n",
        "- describe selective prediction, abstention, and uncertainty-aware deployment;\n",
        "- connect mathematical clarity with responsible AI-assisted NLP practice.\n",
        "\n",
        "## 25.2 The statistical object behind a responsible NLP system\n",
        "\n",
        "Let $X$ denote an input text, such as a document, prompt, search query, or conversation.  Let $Y$ denote the target variable, such as a class label, relevance judgment, next token, toxicity rating, or answer-quality score.  Let $A$ denote a group attribute or context variable relevant for auditing.  Depending on the application, $A$ may represent language variety, geography, institution type, dialect, writing style, document source, demographic group, or another variable that may affect model behavior.\n",
        "\n",
        "A responsible statistical analysis begins with a joint distribution\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "P(X,A,Y).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "A trained NLP model is a function, or a conditional distribution, built from data:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "f_\\theta:X \\mapsto \\widehat{Y},\n",
        "\\qquad\n",
        "p_\\theta(y\\mid x).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "A deployment decision is usually not just the model output.  It also includes a threshold, abstention rule, retrieval rule, or human-review rule.  We therefore write the full deployed rule as\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "d(x)=\\text{decision made after observing }x.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "A performance claim has mathematical meaning only relative to a distribution and a loss.  If $L(d(X),Y)$ is the loss, then the population risk is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "R(d)=E_P[L(d(X),Y)].\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The empirical risk computed on a test set is an estimator:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\widehat{R}(d)=\\frac{1}{n}\\sum_{i=1}^n L(d(x_i),y_i).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The key point is simple but important: **a metric is not an ethical conclusion**.  A metric is a statistic computed under assumptions.\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.math-card}\n",
        "**Responsible NLP as a mathematical workflow.**\n",
        "\n",
        "1. Specify the population $P(X,A,Y)$.\n",
        "2. Specify the model or decision rule $d$.\n",
        "3. Specify the loss $L$.\n",
        "4. Estimate performance and uncertainty.\n",
        "5. Audit performance conditionally on relevant subpopulations.\n",
        "6. Study behavior under distribution shift.\n",
        "7. Decide whether the system should be deployed, constrained, redesigned, or rejected.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "## 25.3 Sources of bias in NLP\n",
        "\n",
        "The word **bias** has several meanings.  In statistics, bias often refers to\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\operatorname{Bias}(\\widehat{\\theta})=E[\\widehat{\\theta}]-\\theta.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "In responsible NLP, bias is broader.  It often refers to systematic error, unequal performance, stereotyped representation, or harmful behavior.  These notions are related, but not identical.\n",
        "\n",
        "A useful mathematical decomposition is the following.\n",
        "\n",
        "| Source | Mathematical form | NLP example |\n",
        "|---|---|---|\n",
        "| Sampling bias | $P_{\\text{train}}(X,A,Y) \\ne P_{\\text{target}}(X,A,Y)$ | training corpus overrepresents certain genres |\n",
        "| Measurement bias | observed $\\widetilde{Y}$ differs from intended $Y$ | noisy toxicity labels or vague annotation instructions |\n",
        "| Representation bias | $\\phi(X)$ loses relevant information | tokenization fails for names, dialects, or multilingual text |\n",
        "| Label bias | $P(\\widetilde{Y}\\mid X,A)$ reflects annotator/systematic bias | same text rated differently depending on dialect |\n",
        "| Optimization bias | objective weights some errors more than others | minimizing average loss hides subgroup error |\n",
        "| Deployment bias | use differs from training/evaluation assumptions | classifier used as a gatekeeping tool outside original context |\n",
        "| Feedback bias | future data depends on earlier model decisions | search ranking affects what users click next |\n",
        "\n",
        "For MA Applied Math and MS Statistics students, the important habit is to ask:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\text{Which distribution generated the data, and which distribution will use the model?}\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "If those distributions differ, a test-set score may not estimate deployment behavior.\n",
        "\n",
        "## 25.4 Group fairness metrics\n",
        "\n",
        "Consider a binary decision problem with label $Y\\in\\{0,1\\}$ and prediction $\\widehat{Y}\\in\\{0,1\\}$.  Suppose $A$ is a group variable.  For a fixed group $a$, define:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\operatorname{TPR}_a=P(\\widehat{Y}=1\\mid Y=1,A=a),\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\operatorname{FPR}_a=P(\\widehat{Y}=1\\mid Y=0,A=a),\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\operatorname{PPV}_a=P(Y=1\\mid \\widehat{Y}=1,A=a),\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\operatorname{SR}_a=P(\\widehat{Y}=1\\mid A=a),\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "where TPR is true positive rate, FPR is false positive rate, PPV is positive predictive value, and SR is selection rate.\n",
        "\n",
        "Common fairness criteria include:\n",
        "\n",
        "| Criterion | Mathematical condition | Interpretation |\n",
        "|---|---|---|\n",
        "| Demographic parity | $P(\\widehat{Y}=1\\mid A=a)=P(\\widehat{Y}=1\\mid A=b)$ | equal selection rates |\n",
        "| Equal opportunity | $P(\\widehat{Y}=1\\mid Y=1,A=a)=P(\\widehat{Y}=1\\mid Y=1,A=b)$ | equal true positive rates |\n",
        "| Equalized odds | $\\widehat{Y}\\perp A\\mid Y$ | equal TPR and FPR |\n",
        "| Predictive parity | $P(Y=1\\mid \\widehat{Y}=1,A=a)=P(Y=1\\mid \\widehat{Y}=1,A=b)$ | equal precision among selected cases |\n",
        "| Calibration by group | $P(Y=1\\mid S=s,A=a)=s$ | scores mean the same probability in each group |\n",
        "\n",
        "Here $S\\in[0,1]$ is a model score, such as a predicted probability.\n",
        "\n",
        "These criteria are not merely technical.  They encode different normative choices.  Equalizing selection rates, equalizing error rates, and equalizing predictive values may lead to different thresholds and different social consequences.\n",
        "\n",
        "## 25.5 Why fairness metrics can conflict\n",
        "\n",
        "Suppose the base rates differ:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "P(Y=1\\mid A=a)\\ne P(Y=1\\mid A=b).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "If a classifier is calibrated within each group and is not perfect, then it is generally impossible to also equalize all error rates across groups.  This is not a paradox.  It is a constraint imposed by conditional probability.\n",
        "\n",
        "To see the tension, write the selection rate for group $a$ as\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "P(\\widehat{Y}=1\\mid A=a)\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\operatorname{TPR}_a P(Y=1\\mid A=a)\n",
        "+\n",
        "\\operatorname{FPR}_a P(Y=0\\mid A=a).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "If the base rate $P(Y=1\\mid A=a)$ changes across groups, then equal TPR and equal FPR generally imply different selection rates.  Conversely, forcing equal selection rates may require unequal error rates.\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.callout-important}\n",
        "Fairness metrics should not be selected mechanically.  In a real application, one must ask what the decision means, what errors cost, who is affected, what appeal process exists, and whether the model should be used at all.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "## 25.6 Confusion matrices by group\n",
        "\n",
        "For a fixed group $a$, the confusion matrix counts are\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\begin{array}{c|cc}\n",
        "& Y=1 & Y=0 \\\\\n",
        "\\hline\n",
        "\\widehat{Y}=1 & TP_a & FP_a \\\\\n",
        "\\widehat{Y}=0 & FN_a & TN_a\n",
        "\\end{array}\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "From these counts,\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\operatorname{TPR}_a=\\frac{TP_a}{TP_a+FN_a},\n",
        "\\qquad\n",
        "\\operatorname{FPR}_a=\\frac{FP_a}{FP_a+TN_a},\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\operatorname{PPV}_a=\\frac{TP_a}{TP_a+FP_a},\n",
        "\\qquad\n",
        "\\operatorname{SR}_a=\\frac{TP_a+FP_a}{TP_a+FP_a+FN_a+TN_a}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The following Python example computes these quantities."
      ],
