        "## 25.11 Embedding association and representation bias\n",
        "\n",
        "Embedding models represent words, sentences, or documents as vectors.  Bias can appear as geometric association.  Let $X$ and $Y$ be two sets of target words, and let $A$ and $B$ be two sets of attribute words.  A simple association score is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "s(w,A,B)=\\frac{1}{|A|}\\sum_{a\\in A}\\cos(e_w,e_a)\n",
        "-\n",
        "\\frac{1}{|B|}\\sum_{b\\in B}\\cos(e_w,e_b).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "A set-level effect score is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "S(X,Y,A,B)=\\sum_{x\\in X}s(x,A,B)-\\sum_{y\\in Y}s(y,A,B).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "This type of score is inspired by word-embedding association tests and related representation-auditing methods @caliskan_semantics_2017, @bolukbasi_man_is_2016."
      ],
      "id": "0de32b2b"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "# A small toy embedding audit.  These numbers are artificial and for pedagogy only.\n",
        "emb = {\n",
        "    \"nurse\": np.array([0.10, 0.80]),\n",
        "    \"engineer\": np.array([0.80, 0.10]),\n",
        "    \"teacher\": np.array([0.20, 0.70]),\n",
        "    \"programmer\": np.array([0.75, 0.20]),\n",
        "    \"care\": np.array([0.00, 1.00]),\n",
        "    \"family\": np.array([0.10, 0.95]),\n",
        "    \"logic\": np.array([1.00, 0.00]),\n",
        "    \"machine\": np.array([0.95, 0.10]),\n",
        "}\n",
        "\n",
        "def cosine(u, v):\n",
        "    u = np.asarray(u)\n",
        "    v = np.asarray(v)\n",
        "    return float(u @ v / (np.linalg.norm(u) * np.linalg.norm(v)))\n",
        "\n",
        "def assoc(word, A, B):\n",
        "    return np.mean([cosine(emb[word], emb[a]) for a in A]) - np.mean([cosine(emb[word], emb[b]) for b in B])\n",
        "\n",
        "X = [\"nurse\", \"teacher\"]\n",
        "Y = [\"engineer\", \"programmer\"]\n",
        "A = [\"care\", \"family\"]\n",
        "B = [\"logic\", \"machine\"]\n",
        "\n",
        "pd.DataFrame({\n",
        "    \"word\": X + Y,\n",
        "    \"association_to_A_minus_B\": [assoc(w, A, B) for w in X + Y]\n",
        "})"
      ],
      "id": "8390e922",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "A linear debiasing operation can be written as orthogonal projection.  If $b$ is a unit direction representing an unwanted association, then the projection removing that direction is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\Pi_{b^\\perp}(v)=v-(v^Tb)b.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "This operation is mathematically clean, but it is not a complete solution.  Bias may be nonlinear, contextual, or encoded in many directions.  Projection can also remove useful information or create a false sense of safety."
      ],
      "id": "085474e3"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "# Remove the direction from \"logic/machine\" to \"care/family\" in the toy example.\n",
        "mean_A = np.mean([emb[a] for a in A], axis=0)\n",
        "mean_B = np.mean([emb[b] for b in B], axis=0)\n",
        "bias_direction = mean_A - mean_B\n",
        "bias_direction = bias_direction / np.linalg.norm(bias_direction)\n",
        "\n",
        "def remove_direction(v, b):\n",
        "    return v - (v @ b) * b\n",
        "\n",
        "emb_projected = {w: remove_direction(v, bias_direction) for w, v in emb.items()}\n",
        "\n",
        "def assoc_projected(word, A, B):\n",
        "    return np.mean([cosine(emb_projected[word], emb_projected[a]) for a in A]) - np.mean([cosine(emb_projected[word], emb_projected[b]) for b in B])\n",
        "\n",
        "pd.DataFrame({\n",
        "    \"word\": X + Y,\n",
        "    \"before\": [assoc(w, A, B) for w in X + Y],\n",
        "    \"after_projection\": [assoc_projected(w, A, B) for w in X + Y]\n",
        "})"
