        "\n",
        "## 25.13 Privacy and memorization\n",
        "\n",
        "NLP systems can leak information from training data, prompts, logs, or retrieved documents.  A mathematical privacy guarantee should describe how the output distribution changes when one individual's data is changed.\n",
        "\n",
        "A randomized algorithm $M$ is $\\varepsilon$-differentially private if for all neighboring datasets $D$ and $D'$ differing in one individual, and for all measurable output sets $S$,\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "P(M(D)\\in S)\\le e^\\varepsilon P(M(D')\\in S).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Smaller $\\varepsilon$ means stronger privacy.  Differential privacy is not the only privacy concept, but it is mathematically precise @dwork_roth_2014.\n",
        "\n",
        "A simple example is randomized response.  Suppose a private binary answer is $Y\\in\\{0,1\\}$.  The released answer $Z$ equals $Y$ with probability $p$ and is flipped with probability $1-p$.  The privacy parameter is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\varepsilon=\\log\\frac{p}{1-p}.\n",
        "$$"
      ],
      "id": "778bd673"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "ps = np.array([0.55, 0.60, 0.70, 0.80, 0.90])\n",
        "eps = np.log(ps / (1 - ps))\n",
